# wie_validate --keys: 배틀몬스터 (LGT) 새로하기 → 포획 튜토리얼 → 마을 → 집 안.
# Grammar and cap rule: `--keys` help / `parse_keys` in wie_cli/src/bin/wie_validate.rs.
#
#   mkdir -p <dir>   # --shotdir does not create it; a missing dir silently yields 0 shots
#   cargo run -q --release -p wie_cli --bin wie_validate -- --inject --boot-secs 6 \
#     --max-ticks 100000000000 --keys docs/keys/battlemonster-village.keys --shotdir <dir> <game>
#
# Step 97 (`97_OK`) is the village. Step 104 (`104_UP`) is inside the house only at
# low load: a held key walks per tick, not per second, so the tail after the village
# is not load-proof (docs/report/0298: 0/4 once load climbed to 65-129). Target the village.
# The tutorial asks for STAR (43), CLR (47) and HASH (50, 62) by name; its yes/no
# prompt (step 23) cannot be declined with NUM2. The gaps are wide on purpose:
# the fixed 27-key script feeds a 0.6 s grid, and under load the keys meant
# for later screens land on the help screen instead. Key names only — no game bytes.
# Each HASH is preceded by one spare OK. How many OKs a dialogue takes varies between
# runs of the same build and command (10 before the second HASH in this round's runs,
# 11 in the review's; the cause is not pinned), and a HASH pressed one screen early
# is dropped — after which every OK hits the "#키를 눌러봐" screen, where OK does
# nothing, and the run ends in the tutorial (PR #331 review: 0/2, both stuck there).
# The spare OK is safe because of that same fact: on the HASH prompt an OK is a no-op
# (same frame before and after, measured). Not the save state — the validator's
# filesystem and database are in-memory, so every run starts from no save.
# A script is tied to the engine it was recorded on: a fix that lets the game
# go further moves the path (the pre-#306 version of this path skipped the tutorial).
